{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791818873,"nonce":"cZ5YkbZxcuvhxdMT3hLc5elZZhc=","title":"Tree allocation 3/7: support stable generated recursive helpers across execution tiers","message":"Resolve the architectural gap before implementing fusion: AIR currently optimizes one function at a time, while a fused recursive helper needs module-owned identity and recursive references.\n\nWork:\n- Choose and document a module preparation/registration design that makes generated helpers available before dependent function tables and metadata are finalized.\n- Provide deterministic IDs and signatures, recursive placeholders/references, deduplication, and bounded helper/code growth.\n- Include specialization assumptions in cache keys and invalidate on relevant code/type/host metadata changes and hot reload.\n- Ensure interpreter, Cranelift, LLVM, AOT reachability, and serialization can resolve the same helpers.\n- Preserve canonical bodies, source positions, function identity, and OSR execution-state compatibility. Do not independently synthesize incompatible helpers in separate tiers.\n- Keep transformation activation behind an explicit development option.\n\nAcceptance:\n- A small generated recursive helper verifies and runs correctly through the shared pipeline and relevant tier transitions.\n- Tests cover recursion resolution, stable registration, cache/invalidation, fallback, and code-growth limits.\n- The design identifies affected wasm/AOT and debugging contracts; unsupported paths fail conservatively rather than executing mismatched metadata.\n\nCommit this infrastructure checkpoint and link test evidence here.\n\nParent: b002f3fa36f812b67c8cac99068b8183f37520d7e6d61de6caedd54e86bc089f\nDepends on: 05ec58f75916fb34c6c72acf3ee1c89dbb3c8b1764e9559c6185ba3496ae3921","files":null}]}